<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<meta name="color-scheme" content="light dark">
<title>Lag Lens</title>
<meta name="description" content="Lag Lens is a network-lag witness you run on a computer wired to your Xfinity XB7 while you game. It probes the gateway, the Comcast hops and the internet several times a second, classifies lag episodes, and writes a report you can show Comcast.">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
<link rel="icon" href="data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 32 32'%3E%3Ccircle cx='16' cy='16' r='13' fill='none' stroke='%234f8cff' stroke-width='3'/%3E%3Ccircle cx='16' cy='16' r='4' fill='%234f8cff'/%3E%3C/svg%3E">
</head>
<body>
<a class="skip" href="#install">Skip to install</a>

<header class="hero">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">v{{VERSION}} &middot; built {{BUILT_AT}}</p>
    <h1><span class="mark" aria-hidden="true"></span>Lag Lens</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A parallel network-lag witness you run on a computer wired to your Xfinity XB7 while you game.
      It probes the gateway, the Comcast hops and the internet several times a second, detects and
      classifies lag episodes, and writes a self-contained report you can show Comcast.
    </p>
    <p class="hero-actions">
      <a class="btn primary" href="#install">Install in two commands</a>
      <a class="btn" href="lag-lens.zip" download>Download lag-lens.zip</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</header>

<main class="wrap">

<section id="install">
  <h2>Install</h2>
  <p>Open a terminal on the computer that is plugged into the XB7 and run these two commands.</p>

  <div class="cmd">
    <div class="cmd-label">1. Download and install to <code>~/lag-lens</code></div>
    <pre><code id="cmd1">curl -fsSL {{BASE_URL}}/get.sh | sh</code></pre>
    <button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="cmd1">Copy</button>
  </div>

  <div class="cmd">
    <div class="cmd-label">2. Start it</div>
    <pre><code id="cmd2">cd ~/lag-lens &amp;&amp; bash start.sh</code></pre>
    <button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="cmd2">Copy</button>
  </div>

  <p class="muted">
    The installer verifies the download against its SHA-256 before unpacking, keeps a previous install as
    <code>~/lag-lens.old</code>, and carries over your <code>config.json</code> and <code>sessions/</code>.
    No <code>sudo</code>, no <code>pip</code>, nothing is added to your PATH or startup items.
  </p>
</section>

<section id="download">
  <h2>Download</h2>
  <div class="download-card">
    <a class="btn primary big" href="lag-lens.zip" download>Download lag-lens.zip</a>
    <dl class="meta">
      <dt>Version</dt><dd>{{VERSION}}</dd>
      <dt>Built</dt><dd>{{BUILT_AT}}</dd>
      <dt>SHA-256</dt><dd><code class="hash">{{SHA256}}</code></dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="muted">Also: <a href="lag-lens.zip.sha256">lag-lens.zip.sha256</a> &middot; <a href="version.json">version.json</a> &middot; <a href="get.sh">get.sh</a></p>
  </div>

  <h3>Manual install</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Download <code>lag-lens.zip</code> (button above) and unzip it. You get a <code>lag-lens/</code> folder; put it wherever you like, for example your home folder.</li>
    <li>Optional: check the download. <code>shasum -a 256 lag-lens.zip</code> (macOS) or <code>sha256sum lag-lens.zip</code> (Linux) should print the hash shown above.</li>
    <li>Open a terminal in that folder and run <code>bash start.sh</code>.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section id="requirements">
  <h2>Requirements</h2>
  <ul class="checks">
    <li><strong>Linux</strong> (including Bazzite / Fedora, Ubuntu, Debian, Arch) <strong>or macOS</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>python3 3.9 or newer</strong>. macOS ships one (run <code>xcode-select --install</code> if asked); Linux distributions include it.</li>
    <li><strong>No root, no sudo.</strong> Unprivileged ICMP sockets are used; no raw sockets, no capabilities.</li>
    <li><strong>No pip, no packages.</strong> Standard library only; the dashboard is vanilla HTML and JavaScript served from the zip.</li>
    <li><strong>A wired connection to the XB7</strong> gives the cleanest evidence. Wi-Fi works, but Wi-Fi noise is then part of what gets measured (and is flagged as such).</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="what-you-see">
  <h2>What you'll see</h2>
  <div class="grid2">
    <div>
      <h3>In the terminal</h3>
      <p>A quick self-check (which probes work, which gateway was found, whether the XB7 login succeeded), then
      a one-line status ticker that updates every second: gateway, Comcast hop and internet latency, loss, and
      the current verdict. The URL of the dashboard is printed next to a QR code so you can open it on your phone.
      Press <kbd>Enter</kbd> any time lag happens to drop a mark.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>In the browser</h3>
      <p>A dashboard at <code>http://&lt;your-computer&gt;:8787/</code> with live timelines for every target, the
      path ladder (your PC &rarr; XB7 &rarr; Comcast hops &rarr; internet), episode cards that say <em>where</em>
      the lag happened and how confident the classification is, and a big <strong>Lag happened</strong> button.
      A phone-sized page lives at <code>/phone</code>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>When you stop (or every 30 seconds while it runs) Lag Lens writes <code>report.html</code>: a single file with
  the charts, every episode, the XB7 DOCSIS readings, and a plain-English "Tell Comcast" block you can paste into
  a support chat or hand to a technician.</p>
</section>

<section id="troubleshooting">
  <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>

  <details open>
    <summary>macOS: "gateway unreachable" or everything local fails</summary>
    <p>macOS blocks local-network access per app. Allow it for your terminal app:
    <strong>System Settings &rarr; Privacy &amp; Security &rarr; Local Network</strong>, then switch on
    Terminal (or iTerm, Ghostty, WezTerm, Visual Studio Code, whichever you launched it from). Restart Lag Lens
    afterwards. The doctor detects this case and tells you which app to allow.</p>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Bazzite / Steam Deck style Linux</summary>
    <ul>
      <li>Switch to <strong>Desktop Mode</strong>, open Konsole, and run it from <code>~/lag-lens</code> (your home folder is writable and persists across updates; the system image is not).</li>
      <li>If your phone cannot reach the dashboard, the firewall zone probably blocks port 8787. Either run
      <code>sudo firewall-cmd --add-port=8787/tcp</code> for this session, or open the port in the zone your
      Ethernet interface is in. Lag Lens itself never needs root.</li>
      <li>Keep the screen from sleeping while you monitor; Lag Lens asks the system to inhibit sleep, but a lid close still stops the network.</li>
    </ul>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>My phone can't open the dashboard</summary>
    <ol class="checks">
      <li>Phone and computer are on the <strong>same Wi-Fi / same network</strong> (not cellular, not a guest network, no VPN on either side).</li>
      <li>You typed <strong><code>http://</code></strong>, not <code>https://</code>. Browsers upgrade to https silently; Lag Lens serves plain http on your LAN only.</li>
      <li>The port is <strong>8787</strong> (or the next free one; the terminal prints the exact URL). Try the IP address the terminal shows rather than a hostname.</li>
      <li>Scan the QR code in the terminal or on the dashboard instead of typing the address.</li>
      <li>On Linux, check the firewall (see the Bazzite note above). On macOS, if a third-party firewall is installed, allow incoming connections for python3.</li>
    </ol>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>It says python3 is too old or missing</summary>
    <p>Lag Lens needs Python 3.9 or newer. On macOS, <code>xcode-select --install</code> provides it. If you
    installed a newer Python with Homebrew, <code>start.sh</code> prefers it automatically; you can force one with
    <code>LAGLENS_PYTHON=/path/to/python3 bash start.sh</code>.</p>
  </details>
</section>

<section id="privacy">
  <h2>Privacy</h2>
  <p>Nothing phones home. The only traffic Lag Lens generates is the probes it is there to measure: ICMP and TCP
  to your gateway, the Comcast hops on your path, Cloudflare and Google anycast addresses, Xbox Live sign-in
  hosts, NTP, and small CDN fetches. There is no telemetry, no account, and no update is ever installed without
  you running <code>bash start.sh --update</code>.</p>
  <p>If you let it read your XB7's DOCSIS stats, the gateway password stays in <code>config.json</code> on your
  machine (file mode 600) and is only ever sent to the gateway itself. It is redacted from logs, the dashboard
  and the report. Reports and exports are files on your disk; you decide who sees them.</p>
</section>

